From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PATCH] serial: a modem-status change with no tty open

This is the suspend/resume panic. Measured on the device on 2026-10-23 with
V81: the reader put it to sleep, and about two minutes later it restarted
itself. The capture (`android/logs/20261023T181451-6166/previous-kernel.txt`)
holds the whole chain:

    PM: late suspend of devices complete after 0.387 msecs
    PM: early resume of devices complete after 0.203 msecs
    remove wake up source irq 51
    Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 00000088
    Internal error: Oops: 5 [#1] PREEMPT
    PC is at tty_ldisc_try+0x30/0xa8
    LR is at tty_ldisc_ref+0x10/0x14
    r0 : 00000000
    Backtrace:
    [<c02ab958>] (tty_ldisc_try+0x0/0xa8) from [<c02aba10>] (tty_ldisc_ref+0x10/0x14)
    [<c02aba00>] (tty_ldisc_ref+0x0/0x14) from [<c02c2060>] (imx_timeout+0x130/0x244)
    [<c02c1f30>] (imx_timeout+0x0/0x244) from [<c007dd74>] (run_timer_softirq+0xf8/0x298)
    Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception in interrupt

`imx_timeout()` is the UART's modem-status timer (every MCTRL_TIMEOUT, 250 ms).
It calls `imx_mctrl_check()`, which the compiler inlines, and that calls
`uart_handle_dcd_change()` when carrier detect has changed -- an inline helper
in this header, which hands `port->tty` straight to `tty_ldisc_ref()`.
`tty_ldisc_try()` then does `test_bit(TTY_LDISC, &tty->flags)`, and
`offsetof(struct tty_struct, flags)` is 0x88 on this build, which is the
address in the oops.

`port->tty` is NULL whenever no tty is open on the port, and on this machine
that is the normal state of the console: PID 1 opens `/dev/console` with
O_CLOEXEC, so the descriptor closes when it execs Android's init, the tty's
open count reaches zero and `uart_shutdown()` clears `state->port.tty`. The
port keeps its `uart_state` either way -- `imx_timeout()`'s own guard is
`if (sport->port.state)`, which is never false once the driver is registered --
so nothing between the timer and the dereference notices that the tty is gone.

The helper already knows this can happen. Thirteen lines below the unguarded
`tty_ldisc_ref(port->tty)` stands `else if (port->tty) tty_hangup(port->tty);`.
One branch tests it and the first line does not. Mainline later took the tty
into a local and made every use of it conditional, which is what this does:

  * `uart_handle_dcd_change()` takes the reference only when there is a tty,
    and passes that local to `dcd_change()` rather than re-reading the field;
  * `uart_handle_cts_change()` touches `tty->hw_stopped` only when there is
    one. Reaching it needs ASYNC_CTS_FLOW, which needs an open tty, so this
    half has not been seen to fire -- it is the same mistake in the same
    helper and is corrected while the file is open.

Fixing it here rather than in the i.MX driver is deliberate: the fault is the
helper dereferencing a field it documents as optional, and every serial driver
that reports a modem-status change goes through these two functions.

Why it only showed on a resume: with no tty the timer should not be running at
all -- `imx_shutdown()` deletes it. Something arms it again across the suspend,
and `get_mctrl()` on a port that was just re-initialised reports a changed DCD,
so the first firing after the resume is the one that walks into this. That
arming is a separate question (the UART waking the CPU four times a second for
a port nobody has open is worth its own measurement); this patch is about the
dereference, which must not crash whatever arms the timer.

--- a/include/linux/serial_core.h
+++ b/include/linux/serial_core.h
@@ -532,7 +532,10 @@
 {
 	struct uart_state *state = uport->state;
 	struct tty_port *port = &state->port;
-	struct tty_ldisc *ld = tty_ldisc_ref(port->tty);
+	struct tty_struct *tty = port->tty;
+	/* NULL whenever nothing has the port open, which is this machine's
+	 * console most of the time. */
+	struct tty_ldisc *ld = tty ? tty_ldisc_ref(tty) : NULL;
 	struct pps_event_time ts;
 
 	if (ld && ld->ops->dcd_change)
@@ -547,12 +550,12 @@
 	if (port->flags & ASYNC_CHECK_CD) {
 		if (status)
 			wake_up_interruptible(&port->open_wait);
-		else if (port->tty)
-			tty_hangup(port->tty);
+		else if (tty)
+			tty_hangup(tty);
 	}
 
 	if (ld && ld->ops->dcd_change)
-		ld->ops->dcd_change(port->tty, status, &ts);
+		ld->ops->dcd_change(tty, status, &ts);
 	if (ld)
 		tty_ldisc_deref(ld);
 }
@@ -570,7 +573,7 @@
 
 	uport->icount.cts++;
 
-	if (port->flags & ASYNC_CTS_FLOW) {
+	if (tty && (port->flags & ASYNC_CTS_FLOW)) {
 		if (tty->hw_stopped) {
 			if (status) {
 				tty->hw_stopped = 0;
